### What are the five knobs for throughput?

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class HighThroughputConfig {
    public static Properties config() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "broker1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");

        // Throughput knobs
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BATCH_SIZE_CONFIG, 65536);          // 64 KB
        props.put(ProducerConfig.LINGER_MS_CONFIG, 10);              // 10 ms
        props.put(ProducerConfig.COMPRESSION_TYPE_CONFIG, "lz4");    // fast codec
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");                // keep safe
        props.put(ProducerConfig.MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS_PER_CONNECTION, 5);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, "true"); // ordering safe
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BUFFER_MEMORY_CONFIG, 67108864);    // 64 MB

        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties p = config();
        System.out.println("High-throughput producer config:");
        System.out.println("  batch.size     = 64 KB");
        System.out.println("  linger.ms      = 10 ms");
        System.out.println("  compression    = lz4");
        System.out.println("  acks           = all");
        System.out.println("  max.in.flight  = 5");
        System.out.println("  buffer.memory  = 64 MB");
    }
}
```

```text
High-throughput producer config:
  batch.size     = 64 KB
  linger.ms      = 10 ms
  compression    = lz4
  acks           = all
  max.in.flight  = 5
  buffer.memory  = 64 MB
```

### How does batch.size work?

The batch.size setting (default 16,384 bytes) controls how many bytes accumulate per partition before sending. Larger batches mean fewer network requests and better compression.

```java
package com.bank.throughput;

public class BatchSizeEffect {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int recordSize = 200;  // bytes per transfer event
        int[] batchSizes = {16384, 32768, 65536};

        for (int batchSize : batchSizes) {
            int recordsPerBatch = batchSize / recordSize;
            System.out.printf("  batch.size=%d KB -> ~%d records/batch%n",
                batchSize / 1024, recordsPerBatch);
        }
        System.out.println("  Fewer, larger batches = fewer network requests");
    }
}
```

```text
  batch.size=16 KB -> ~81 records/batch
  batch.size=32 KB -> ~163 records/batch
  batch.size=64 KB -> ~327 records/batch
  Fewer, larger batches = fewer network requests
```

### Why is linger.ms essential?

With linger.ms=0 (default), the producer sends as soon as it can. Under light load, batches ship half-empty. A small linger of 5-20 ms lets many records arrive in that window, filling batches efficiently.

```java
package com.bank.throughput;

public class LingerEffect {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("linger.ms=0 (default):");
        System.out.println("  Batch sent immediately, often half-empty");
        System.out.println("  Many small requests -> low throughput");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("linger.ms=10:");
        System.out.println("  Wait up to 10ms to fill the batch");
        System.out.println("  Fewer, fuller batches -> high throughput");
        System.out.println("  Cost: up to 10ms added latency per record");
    }
}
```

```text
linger.ms=0 (default):
  Batch sent immediately, often half-empty
  Many small requests -> low throughput

linger.ms=10:
  Wait up to 10ms to fill the batch
  Fewer, fuller batches -> high throughput
  Cost: up to 10ms added latency per record
```

### How does compression reinforce batching?

Kafka compresses the whole batch, not individual records. A larger batch compresses better because more repetitive content is available.

```java
package com.bank.throughput;

public class CompressionBatching {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Batch of 10 JSON transfer events:");
        System.out.println("  Uncompressed : ~2000 bytes");
        System.out.println("  lz4 compressed: ~600 bytes (70% savings)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Batch of 300 JSON transfer events:");
        System.out.println("  Uncompressed : ~60000 bytes");
        System.out.println("  lz4 compressed: ~8000 bytes (87% savings)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Larger batch + compression = multiplicative savings");
    }
}
```

```text
Batch of 10 JSON transfer events:
  Uncompressed : ~2000 bytes
  lz4 compressed: ~600 bytes (70% savings)

Batch of 300 JSON transfer events:
  Uncompressed : ~60000 bytes
  lz4 compressed: ~8000 bytes (87% savings)

Larger batch + compression = multiplicative savings
```

### How does max.in.flight.requests.per.connection work?

This setting (default 5) controls how many unacknowledged batches can be in flight. Higher values mean the producer does not wait for each batch to be acknowledged. With idempotence enabled, ordering is preserved up to 5 in-flight requests.

```java
package com.bank.throughput;

public class InFlightEffect {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("max.in.flight = 1:");
        System.out.println("  Send batch, wait for ack, send next");
        System.out.println("  Throughput limited by round-trip time");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("max.in.flight = 5 (with idempotence):");
        System.out.println("  5 batches in flight simultaneously");
        System.out.println("  Throughput multiplied");
        System.out.println("  Ordering preserved (idempotence reorders on retry)");
    }
}
```

```text
max.in.flight = 1:
  Send batch, wait for ack, send next
  Throughput limited by round-trip time

max.in.flight = 5 (with idempotence):
  5 batches in flight simultaneously
  Throughput multiplied
  Ordering preserved (idempotence reorders on retry)
```

### How do the knobs interact? (comparison)

| Knob | Default | Throughput value | Trade-off |
|---|---|---|---|
| batch.size | 16 KB | 64 KB+ | More memory per partition |
| linger.ms | 0 | 5-20 ms | Adds latency per record |
| compression.type | none | lz4 or zstd | CPU cost (minimal) |
| acks | all | all (keep it!) | No trade-off -- use batching instead |
| max.in.flight | 5 | 5 | >5 risks reordering without idempotence |
| buffer.memory | 32 MB | 64-128 MB | JVM heap pressure |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class ThroughputMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: big batch.size with linger.ms=0
        System.out.println("WRONG: batch.size=64KB but linger.ms=0");
        System.out.println("  Batches ship half-empty under light load");
        System.out.println();

        // WRONG: weakening acks for throughput
        System.out.println("WRONG: acks=0 for speed in banking");
        System.out.println("  Silent data loss is unacceptable");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: keep acks=all, optimize batching and compression
        System.out.println("CORRECT: acks=all + batch=64KB + linger=10 + lz4");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: batch.size=64KB but linger.ms=0
  Batches ship half-empty under light load

WRONG: acks=0 for speed in banking
  Silent data loss is unacceptable

CORRECT: acks=all + batch=64KB + linger=10 + lz4
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

These settings interact: a big batch.size is useless without linger.ms. Compression amplifies the benefit of larger batches. More partitions allow more parallelism on both sides. You should also raise buffer.memory if the producer accumulates many batches. Topic design (partition count) is part of throughput tuning.
